title: keybindings panel
tags: Panel [[Panel types]] [[Customising the GUI]] Keybindings
kind: reference
summary: The editor of keybindings.toml: one row per line of the file, its key as written beside the keys it binds, the commands no key runs, a filter by class, and the variables.

Open it with `panel:open here keybindings` (<<key "panel:open here keybindings" "t k">>).
It edits `gui/keybindings.toml` ([[Changing keybindings]]) — what used to be the lower
half of the [[config panel]] —, so a key can be looked up while the panel it acts in
stays on screen.

!! The lines

The list has one row per ''line'' of the file — one binding of a key, as the file
writes it —, in the order of the file, then the keys suggested by a panel or by
`commands.js`, then the commands no key runs. Each row shows:

* its [[class|Command classes]], `?` when `command-classes.toml` gives it none;
* its key ''as written'', variables left in (`{go} l`) — a field: what is typed there is checked as it is typed, ''Enter'' saves the line under the new key, ''Escape'' gives the old one back;
* the keys it really binds, variables replaced: a `{target}` template binds four (`t l`, `i t l`, `b t l`, `n t l`), less the ones a literal line of the file overrides ([[Keybinding variables]]); each one says, on hover, what it runs;
* the command as written, `{target}` left in, and its description;
* where it applies — `global`, a panel type, or `@name` for a text field, followed by `⌨` when it also applies while typing ([[Keybindings]]) —, and ''suggested'' for a key that is not in the file. A suggested key cannot be moved and has no ''Remove''; its key field is read-only.

''Edit'' copies a line into the form; ''Save'' then replaces it — in its place in the
file when it keeps its key and its scope, else the old line goes and the new one is
written at the end. ''Remove'' removes the line, every key it binds. A command with no
key has ''Bind'', which puts it into the form.

!! The classes

The buttons above the list — `A` `B` `C` `D` `✕` and `?` — keep the commands of the
classes pressed ([[Command classes]]): with `D` and `✕` alone, the list is the
commands that have no key, as they should. The file is read each time the list is,
so an edit of it shows at `view:refresh`; one that cannot be read is said, and the
rest is shown unclassed.

!! The form

The form — key, command, `when`, `focus`, `target`, `text-input` — both writes a line
and searches the list:

* the ''key'' typed keeps the lines binding a key it starts: `g` keeps those of `g g` and `g t`, `ctrl` every ctrl chord, and `shift+ctrl+a` finds `ctrl+shift+a`; typed with a `{`, it keeps the lines written with it — `{go` the domain's;
* the ''command'' typed keeps the lines whose command contains it, below those of the key, and the commands with no key it names. The field completes over every command, and the line under the form names the command it reads, with its description.

The variables are listed above the lines. `target` is for a command holding
`{target}`: where its own key aims (`here`, `other`, `new`).

As the form is typed, the panel asks the GUI what a save would refuse, and says it
under the form: a command that does not exist, an unknown variable, a key already
bound in that place — whose row is marked —, or a key that would hide a sequence bound
there or be hidden by it ([[Changing keybindings]]). ''Save'' stays disabled until
nothing does; Enter in a field saves. A literal key over one a template binds is no
refusal but an override, which the line says.

!! The variables

Above the list, every variable of the file, in its order: its name, its value — a
field —, the keys it stands for, and how many lines use it, directly or through
another variable ([[Keybinding variables]]). What a new value would do is said as it
is typed — how many keys change, or why it is refused: a key bound twice, or a key
alone hiding a sequence that nothing hid before. ''Enter'' writes it, in its line of
the file; ''Escape'' gives the old value back.

When the panel is shown, the key field takes the focus, and the list is read again:
what was bound meanwhile, by `config:reload keybindings` or a panel, appears.
`view:refresh` reads it again too.
